\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.25}{\ignorespaces Qualitative evaluation showing tumor localization (confidence score 0.87) on an axial CT slice.}}{122}{figure.caption.106}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.26}{\ignorespaces Example of an interpretable YOLO detection result on a chest CT image. The model localizes a suspected lesion using a bounding box and reports a confidence score of 0.87. Such spatial predictions provide inherent interpretability by explicitly identifying the anatomical region associated with the model's decision, reducing the need for post-hoc visualization techniques.}}{123}{figure.caption.107}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.27}{\ignorespaces Detailed architectural diagram of the YOLOv8 object detection framework, illustrating the Backbone, Neck (FPN + PAN), and Detection Head stages for multi-scale prediction.}}{126}{figure.caption.113}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.28}{\ignorespaces Overall architecture of the proposed Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) model. The user query is transformed into vector embeddings, relevant medical knowledge is retrieved from the FAISS vector database, and the retrieved context is supplied to the Qwen 2.5 language model to generate a grounded and source-aware response.}}{134}{figure.caption.119}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.29}{\ignorespaces Query processing workflow of the proposed RAG system.}}{136}{figure.caption.121}%
